+# SPAKE2+ PAKE driver interface review notes
+
+This note records the interface decision for the initial Matter P-256 SPAKE2+
+implementation. It is intended to accompany the implementation patches and to
+make the driver-facing changes easier to review.
+
+## Decision
+
+Carry SPAKE2+ through the existing PAKE driver path instead of adding a
+software-only side path in `core/psa_crypto.c`.
+
+The initial built-in implementation remains limited to transparent software keys
+and `PSA_ALG_SPAKE2P_MATTER` with
+`PSA_PAKE_PRIMITIVE(PSA_PAKE_PRIMITIVE_TYPE_ECC, PSA_ECC_FAMILY_SECP_R1, 256)`,
+but the core still dispatches through the PAKE driver entry points. Opaque keys
+and external accelerator implementations can therefore be added later by filling
+in the same PAKE driver contract instead of redesigning the core state machine.
+
+## Why this matches TF-PSA-Crypto architecture
+
+TF-PSA-Crypto is organized around the PSA driver interface so that cryptographic
+implementations can be substituted by built-in software, accelerators, secure
+elements or other backends. SPAKE2+ should follow that model even though the
+first merged implementation is built-in-only.
+
+This patch series therefore extends the private PAKE driver inputs rather than
+calling `drivers/builtin/src/psa_crypto_spake2p.c` directly from the public PSA
+layer. The public PSA API is unchanged.
+
+## Driver-input changes under review
+
+The SPAKE2+ patches extend `psa_crypto_driver_pake_inputs_t` with data that is
+needed by augmented PAKEs:
+
+- the raw PAKE key buffer and length;
+- the PSA key attributes for the PAKE key;
+- the selected PAKE role;
+- optional user and peer identities;
+- optional context;
+- explicit presence flags for user, peer, context and role.
+
+The explicit flags are required because SPAKE2+ treats absent and empty inputs
+differently in one important case: absent context is omitted from the transcript,
+while explicitly supplied zero-length context is included as a zero-length field.
+The same flags also preserve zero-length user and peer identities without
+confusing them with "setter not called".
+
+## Compatibility assessment
+
+### Public API and ABI
+
+No public PSA function prototype changes are introduced. The feature is exposed
+through existing PAKE entry points and existing SPAKE2+ identifiers.
+
+### Driver ABI
+
+The affected structure is part of the driver-facing/private implementation
+contract, not the public PSA application ABI. Existing external PAKE drivers may
+need source updates if they instantiate or inspect `psa_crypto_driver_pake_inputs_t`.
+That is acceptable for a new algorithm family, but it should be called out in the
+pull-request description.
+
+### Existing J-PAKE behavior
+
+J-PAKE should remain behaviorally unchanged:
+
+- password and password-hash keys are still used for J-PAKE;
+- the existing J-PAKE step sequence remains intact;
+- the new augmented-PAKE fields are populated only for SPAKE2+ paths;
+- the new user/peer/context presence flags are compatible with the existing
+  non-empty J-PAKE identity requirements.
+
+## Maintainer review checklist
+
+Reviewers should explicitly check the following items before merge:
+
+1. **Layering:** `core/psa_crypto.c` performs public PSA validation and state
+   sequencing, while the built-in driver performs protocol-specific SPAKE2+
+   arithmetic, transcript construction and confirmation.
+2. **Driver extensibility:** the augmented-PAKE fields are sufficient for a
+   future accelerator to implement Matter SPAKE2+ without adding a second public
+   PSA path.
+3. **Algorithm gating:** all SPAKE2+ paths are guarded by
+   `PSA_WANT_ALG_SPAKE2P_MATTER` and the built-in implementation is guarded by
+   `MBEDTLS_PSA_BUILTIN_ALG_SPAKE2P` or
+   `MBEDTLS_PSA_BUILTIN_ALG_SPAKE2P_MATTER` as appropriate.
+4. **Key restrictions:** the first implementation rejects opaque/external keys
+   unless an accelerator path explicitly supports them.
+5. **Context semantics:** omitted context and explicit empty context are tested
+   separately and remain distinguishable through driver dispatch.
+6. **Error mapping:** invalid key-share input maps to
+   `PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT`; confirmation mismatch maps to
+   `PSA_ERROR_INVALID_SIGNATURE`; wrong sequencing maps to
+   `PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE`.
+7. **Secret lifetime:** `w0`, `w1`, ephemeral scalars, transcript-derived keys,
+   confirmation keys and shared secret material are zeroized on abort and failure
+   paths.
+8. **J-PAKE regression:** the J-PAKE PAKE suite passes with and without
+   `PSA_WANT_ALG_SPAKE2P_MATTER` enabled.
+
+## Open follow-ups
+
+These are intentionally out of scope for the first Matter P-256 merge:
+
+- generic RFC 9383 `PSA_ALG_SPAKE2P_HMAC()` and `PSA_ALG_SPAKE2P_CMAC()`;
+- P-384, P-521, edwards25519 and edwards448;
+- persistent storage optimizations for caching `L` with key pairs;
+- opaque-key and external-driver SPAKE2+ implementations;
+- broader conformance/fuzz jobs beyond the focused Matter P-256 CI job.
